  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1144 PM CST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nToday - Friday: Cool and Dry\n\nAn upper level low is located in western Missouri based current \nwater vapor imagery. This low will shift to the east during the day \nthen shoot off to the northeast on Friday. The forecasted path for \nthis low has continued to trend further south keeping any \nprecipitation chances for today south of the forecast area and \nleading to a dry Thanksgiving. Skies remain mostly cloudy with light \nnorth winds for your Thanksgiving. Temperatures stay on the mild \nside with highs in the mid 40s and overnight lows in the 20s.\n\nThe Weekend: Light Precipitation Chances\n\nA secondary, quick moving, trough is expected to move east from the \nNorthern Plains to the Upper Midwest late Friday into early Saturday \nmorning. This system will provide ample lift, associated with a weak \nfront that moves through, to generate light precipitation, if enough \nmoisture is in place. Forecast soundings differ a little in how much \nlow level moisture will be present when lift is most prevalent over \nthe region, however not much is expected for total precipitation \namounts as PWATs are only between 0.4 and 0.6\". As a result, any \nprecipitation that does occur should be light. Given the cooler \ntemperatures throughout the atmospheric column, we cannot rule out a \nwintry mix of precipitation rather than just a light, cold rain. The \nlight precipitation potential should quickly wane beyond sunrise as \nthe lift provided by the trough exits off to the east. For the rest \nof Saturday, decreasing cloud cover and southwesterly to westerly \nwinds should allow for temperatures to rise up into the mid 40s to \nlower 50s for highs.\n\nAnother trough should dig south over the Plains before quickly \nlifting northeast towards New England on Sunday. This system may \nlead to further chances for precipitation from the Plains to the \nMidwest on Sunday. Recent forecast trends continue to show most of \nthe precipitation staying south of the Driftless Region. The 25/12z \nforecast model cluster analysis depicts this trend very well, with 3 \nof the 4 clusters of the LREF indicating very little to no rainfall \nfor the 50th percentile of each cluster. In fact, the cluster with \nthe highest rain amounts is the one with the least amount of members \n(only 15% of the total ensemble). This is a stark difference \ncompared to the 25/00z guidance, which had at least widespread light \nprecipitation in over half of the ensemble output. AI driven \nforecast models are also on this trend (the EC-AIFS always favored a \nsouthern solution) so confidence in widespread precipitation on \nSunday is decreasing. Current NBM PoPs have reflected this southward \nshift and now show a 20 to 40% chance for precipitation for areas \nsouth of I-90. If any precipitation does occur, it would likely be a \ncold, light rain. Temperatures on Sunday should drop back down to \nnear or just below normal behind a cold frontal passage.\n\nNext Week - Seasonal and Dry\n\nAfter the weekend, zonal upper level flow is expected to prevail \nover the region through midweek. This pattern is not favorable for \nprecipitation so dry conditions are currently anticipated beyond \nSunday. The next chance for widespread precipitation (15 to 25%) is \nThursday as a shortwave comes down into the Upper Midwest. After a \nbrief cool down on Sunday due to the trough passing overhead, \ntemperatures should slowly rise back up to above normal during the \nfirst half of next week.",
